A = {
'diarrhea-food-poisoning-2569': dict(
  title='Diarrhoea and Food Poisoning: When Should You See a Doctor? (2026 Update)',
  excerpt='Diarrhoea is currently the most commonly reported illness according to Thailand’s Department of Disease Control. How to care for yourself at home, which danger signs need a doctor, and treatment at HappiWell Clinic with the Gold Card.',
  seo_title='Diarrhoea & Food Poisoning: When to See a Doctor (2026) | HappiWell',
  seo_description='Diarrhoea and food poisoning self-care at home and the danger signs that need a doctor. Treatment at HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok, including with the Gold Card.',
  blocks=[
    'Almost everyone has had diarrhoea, and many people think it is no big deal and will pass. But diarrhoea is currently the most commonly reported illness according to Thailand’s Department of Disease Control, and in some cases it can become serious enough to need a hospital stay if not managed properly. This article explains how diarrhoea and food poisoning differ, how to care for yourself at home and which signs mean it is time to see a doctor.',
    'The current diarrhoea situation in Thailand',
    'The Department of Disease Control, Ministry of Public Health, reported that over the past month (14 June – 9 July 2026) <b>diarrhoea was the most commonly reported illness</b>, ahead of flu, pneumonia, COVID-19 and food poisoning, which together made up the top five for the same period. This shows diarrhoea is closer to home than many people think, especially in the rainy season, when food and water spoil more easily.',
    'How do diarrhoea and food poisoning differ?',
    '“Food poisoning” is caused by eating food or water contaminated with germs or toxins, leading to diarrhoea, stomach pain, nausea and vomiting. “Diarrhoea” is a broader symptom that can be caused by food poisoning, viral infections such as norovirus, or other causes. Both share similar basic care and danger signs.',
    'Common causes in Thailand',
    ['Seafood that is not fresh or not fully cooked', 'Som tam (papaya salad) or yam (spicy salads) made with substandard fermented fish sauce (pla ra) or raw seafood', 'Dishes made with coconut milk, such as curries or desserts topped with coconut cream, which spoil easily in hot, humid weather', 'Unclean ice or drinking water', 'Not washing hands before eating'],
    'Basic care at home',
    ['Sip oral rehydration solution (ORS) often to prevent dehydration — <b>not sports drinks</b>, which are high in sugar and not suitable for diarrhoea', 'Eat soft, easy-to-digest food and avoid fatty or spicy food', 'Get enough rest and avoid strenuous activity', 'Do not immediately buy anti-diarrhoea medicine, especially if you also have a fever, because sometimes the body needs to clear the germs first'],
    'Danger signs: see a doctor quickly',
    ['Watery stools more than 3 times a day for several days without improvement', 'Mucus or blood in the stools', 'High fever', 'Vomiting so severe you cannot keep anything down', 'Signs of severe dehydration, such as feeling faint, palpitations, cold hands and feet, or passing very little urine', 'Pregnant women, children under 5 and people with existing conditions should see a doctor sooner than others, even if symptoms are not yet severe'],
    'How can it be prevented?',
    'Follow the principle of <b>“cooked, hot and clean”</b>: eat freshly cooked, hot and clean food, wash your hands before every meal, avoid seafood that is not fresh or smells unusual, and take extra care with dishes that spoil easily in hot, humid weather, such as coconut milk dishes and raw seafood.',
    'Diarrhoea and food poisoning treatment at HappiWell Clinic',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, a doctor can treat diarrhoea and food poisoning, which are among the conditions <b>covered by the “30 Baht Gold Card — treatment anywhere” scheme</b>. No need to travel to a big hospital. Booking is easy, with no long wait.',
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>How many days of diarrhoea is abnormal?</b><br>Symptoms usually improve within 1–3 days. If they have not improved after 3 days, or are getting worse, see a doctor.',
    '<b>Can I just take anti-diarrhoea medicine?</b><br>Do not take it straight away on your own, especially if you have a fever or blood and mucus in your stools, as it may keep germs or toxins in your body longer. Ask a doctor or pharmacist first.',
    '<b>How is caring for a young child with diarrhoea different?</b><br>Children under 5 become dehydrated much faster than adults. Give them sips of ORS often and watch them closely. If they become drowsy or pass clearly less urine, see a doctor immediately.',
    '<b>Can diarrhoea be treated with the Gold Card?</b><br>Yes. It is one of the common illnesses that a medical clinic like HappiWell can treat under the “30 Baht Gold Card — treatment anywhere” scheme.',
    'Summary',
    'Diarrhoea is currently the most commonly reported illness according to the Department of Disease Control. Most cases can be managed at home with ORS and rest, but if you have danger signs such as blood and mucus in your stools, a high fever or severe dehydration, see a doctor immediately — especially pregnant women, young children and people with existing conditions.',
    'References',
    ['<a href="https://www.hfocus.org/content/2026/07/38793">Hfocus – Top 5 illnesses and causes of death, June–July 2026 (diarrhoea most common) (Thai)</a>', '<a href="https://www.rajavithi.go.th/rj/?p=3728">Rajavithi Hospital – Food poisoning (Thai)</a>', '<a href="https://www.sikarin.com/health/อาหารเป็นพิษ">Sikarin Hospital – Food poisoning (Thai)</a>'],
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. If you have severe symptoms or danger signs, see a doctor immediately.</i>',
  ]),

'diaper-rash-2569': dict(
  title='Diaper Rash in Babies: Causes, Care and When to See a Doctor (2026 Update)',
  excerpt='Almost every baby gets diaper rash. What causes it, how to care for it at home and which signs mean you should see a doctor — with advice from HappiWell Clinic, Thung Khru.',
  seo_title='Diaper Rash in Babies: Causes, Care & When to See a Doctor | HappiWell',
  seo_description='Almost every baby gets diaper rash. Causes, home care and the signs that need a doctor. Advice from HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'You open your baby’s nappy and find a red, angry patch of skin, and many parents are not sure what to do. In fact, diaper rash is the most common skin problem in young children — almost half of babies get it at least once. Most cases can be managed at home, but some need a doctor. This article covers the causes, how to care for it and the signs that it is time to see a doctor.',
    'Types of diaper rash',
    '<b>Irritant diaper rash</b> is the most common type. It is caused by skin being in contact with urine or stools for too long. It usually appears as red patches where the nappy touches the skin, but rarely in the skin folds.',
    '<b>Yeast (Candida) diaper rash</b> is different: it is usually worst in the groin creases and skin folds, with bright red or pink patches with clear edges, sometimes with small spots scattered around the edge. It often follows a course of antibiotics, which kill the bacteria that naturally keep yeast in check.',
    'What causes diaper rash?',
    ['Skin in contact with a wet or soiled nappy for too long', 'Diarrhoea, which means stools touch the skin more often and irritate it more easily', 'Teething — swallowing more saliva can change the stools', 'Starting new foods or solid food', 'Recent antibiotics, which raise the risk of yeast rash', 'Already sensitive skin, such as eczema'],
    'How to help it heal faster at home',
    ['Change the nappy as soon as it is wet or soiled — do not leave it on', 'Clean the skin gently with clean water, avoiding wipes containing alcohol or fragrance', 'Gently pat the skin dry before putting on a new nappy', 'Let the skin get some air, and do not put nappies on too tightly', 'Apply a barrier cream containing zinc oxide before putting on the nappy'],
    'When should you see a doctor?',
    'Most diaper rash improves within 3–4 days with home care, but see a doctor if:',
    ['Your baby has a fever or seems unusually unwell', 'The rash does not improve or gets worse after 2–3 days of home care', 'The rash has pus-filled spots, blisters or oozing', 'The rash is unusually bright red with small spots around the edges (possible yeast)', 'Your baby cries in pain when passing urine or stools', 'The rash spreads beyond the nappy area or looks unusually severe'],
    'Diaper rash advice at HappiWell Clinic',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, a doctor can examine your baby and advise on diaper rash care. If home care has not helped, or you are not sure what type of rash it is, bring your baby in — there is no need to wait until it gets worse.',
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>Does baby powder help prevent diaper rash?</b><br>It is not recommended, because powder can clump in skin folds and cause more irritation. Use a zinc oxide barrier cream instead.',
    '<b>Can diaper rash spread to other children?</b><br>Ordinary irritant rash is not contagious, but if it is caused by yeast, wash your hands thoroughly before and after every nappy change to be safe.',
    '<b>Do cloth nappies reduce diaper rash compared with disposables?</b><br>Both can cause rash if not changed often enough. How often you change the nappy matters more than the type.',
    '<b>At what age are babies most likely to get diaper rash?</b><br>It is most common at 9–12 months, but can happen at any age while a child still wears nappies.',
    'Summary',
    'Diaper rash is a very common skin problem in young children. Most cases can be managed at home by changing nappies often and keeping the skin clean. But if your baby has a fever, the rash does not improve within 2–3 days, or there are pus-filled spots, see a doctor for proper examination and treatment.',
    'References',
    None,
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. See a doctor if you are unsure or the rash does not improve.</i>',
  ]),

'asthma-respiratory-allergy-2569': dict(
  title='Asthma and Respiratory Allergies: Symptoms, Causes and Care (2026 Update)',
  excerpt='Asthma and respiratory allergies: the symptoms, the common triggers, how nebuliser treatment helps, and the price of nebuliser treatment at HappiWell Clinic, Thung Khru.',
  seo_title='Asthma & Respiratory Allergies: Symptoms, Causes & Care (2026)',
  seo_description='Asthma and respiratory allergy symptoms, common triggers, how nebuliser treatment helps, and the price of nebuliser treatment at HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'A chronic cough at night, wheezing, or chest tightness when the weather changes or PM2.5 dust is high — many people dismiss these as an ordinary cold, but they may be signs of asthma or respiratory allergies. This article looks at how serious the condition is, what the triggers are and how nebuliser treatment can help.',
    'What is asthma, and why does the WHO pay close attention to it?',
    'The World Health Organization (WHO) says asthma is the most common chronic non-communicable disease in children and affects both children and adults worldwide. In 2019 there were about 262 million people with asthma worldwide and around 455,000 deaths, mostly in low- and middle-income countries where diagnosis and treatment are not widely available.',
    'Asthma is caused by inflammation and narrowing of the airways, making breathing harder. The good news is that although it is a long-term condition, it can be well controlled with the right medicine and by avoiding triggers.',
    'Common symptoms',
    ['A chronic cough, especially at night', 'Wheezing when breathing out, and sometimes when breathing in', 'Shortness of breath, even at rest', 'Chest tightness and difficulty taking a deep breath'],
    'These symptoms may come and go, and often get worse with a cold or when the weather changes.',
    'Common causes and triggers',
    ['House dust, dust mites, mould and pet hair', 'PM2.5 dust, cigarette smoke and air pollution', 'Pollen from flowers and grass', 'Cold air or frequent weather changes', 'Exercise', 'Strong smells from soap or perfume', 'Stress', 'A family history of allergies or asthma'],
    'How does nebuliser treatment help?',
    'A nebuliser turns liquid medicine into a fine mist so the medicine goes straight into the airways, opening them up and easing breathlessness faster than tablets. It is especially suitable for young children or people who find inhalers difficult to use.',
    'Danger signs: go to hospital immediately',
    ['Severe difficulty breathing, even at rest', 'Being too breathless to speak in full sentences', 'Lips or fingertips turning bluish', 'Symptoms not improving after using an inhaler or bronchodilator'],
    'If you have any of these, go to hospital immediately. Do not wait at home to see how it goes.',
    'Nebuliser treatment at HappiWell Clinic: how much does it cost?',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, our doctors and nurses provide nebuliser treatment. Prices are:',
    ['Nebuliser treatment: <b>200 baht per treatment</b>', 'Nebuliser mask: <b>200 baht</b>, bought once on your first visit and reused for every treatment after that — no need to buy a new one each time'],
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>Can asthma be cured?</b><br>It is usually a long-term condition that can be well controlled with medicine and by avoiding triggers, but it is not completely cured and needs ongoing care.',
    '<b>Will a child with asthma grow out of it?</b><br>Some children improve as they grow, but others continue to have symptoms into adulthood. See a doctor for regular follow-up.',
    '<b>Is frequent nebuliser use harmful?</b><br>Nebuliser treatment as advised by a doctor is safe. Do not change how often or how much you use without talking to a doctor.',
    '<b>Do I need to bring my mask every time?</b><br>Yes. After buying it on your first visit, keep it clean and bring it with you every time you come for treatment.',
    'Summary',
    'Asthma and respiratory allergies are common long-term conditions in both children and adults; the WHO estimates more than 262 million people have asthma worldwide. Although they can be well controlled with medicine, go to hospital immediately if you have severe difficulty breathing or nebuliser treatment does not help. For routine nebuliser treatment, you can visit HappiWell Clinic at an affordable price.',
    'References',
    None,
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. If you have severe difficulty breathing, see a doctor immediately.</i>',
  ]),

'osteoarthritis-2569': dict(
  title='Osteoarthritis and Joint Pain: Symptoms, Causes and Care (2026 Update)',
  excerpt='What osteoarthritis is, its symptoms, how it differs from rheumatoid arthritis, and how to look after your joints so they do not deteriorate quickly — with advice from HappiWell Clinic, Thung Khru.',
  seo_title='Osteoarthritis: Symptoms, Causes & Care (2026) | HappiWell Bangkok',
  seo_description='What osteoarthritis is, its symptoms, how it differs from rheumatoid arthritis and how to slow it down. Advice from HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'Knee pain when getting up from a chair, stiff joints when you wake up, or a grinding sound when you move a joint — many people think these are a normal part of getting older, but they may be signs of osteoarthritis, the most common joint disease. This article explains what osteoarthritis is, which symptoms to look out for and how to stop your joints deteriorating too quickly.',
    'What is osteoarthritis?',
    'Osteoarthritis happens when the cartilage covering the ends of the bones in a joint gradually wears away, until the bones rub directly against each other. It is the most common form of arthritis and usually affects heavily used joints such as the knees, hips, hands and spine.',
    'Common symptoms',
    ['Joint pain, especially during or after using the joint', 'Joint stiffness, most noticeable on waking or after sitting still for a long time', 'Tenderness when pressing on the joint', 'Reduced range of movement', 'A grating feeling or grinding sound when moving the joint', 'Bony lumps around the joint'],
    'How does osteoarthritis differ from rheumatoid arthritis?',
    'A helpful first clue is that <b>osteoarthritis pain usually gets worse with use and better with rest</b>, and morning stiffness usually eases within 30 minutes. Rheumatoid arthritis usually causes morning stiffness lasting more than an hour and often affects several joints on both sides of the body at once. However, an accurate diagnosis still needs a doctor’s examination and X-rays.',
    'Who is at risk of osteoarthritis?',
    ['Older age, especially over 65', 'Being overweight or obese, which increases pressure on weight-bearing joints', 'A previous joint injury', 'Heavy, repetitive use of a joint over many years, for example from work', 'A family history of osteoarthritis'],
    'How to stop your joints deteriorating quickly',
    ['Do low-impact exercise such as swimming, walking or cycling to strengthen the muscles around the joints', 'Keep your weight in a healthy range to reduce pressure on your joints', 'Avoid positions or activities that repeatedly put too much load on the joints', 'For pain, paracetamol or topical pain-relief creams can help as a first step'],
    'When should you see a doctor?',
    'If you have joint pain or stiffness that does not go away, interferes with daily life or keeps coming back more often, see a doctor for an assessment and a care plan. Although osteoarthritis cannot be completely reversed, early care helps slow it down and control pain much better.',
    'Osteoarthritis checks and care at HappiWell Clinic',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, a doctor can assess joint pain and advise on care and suitable medicines — no need to travel to a big hospital. Booking is easy, with no long wait.',
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>Can osteoarthritis be cured?</b><br>Cartilage that has worn away cannot return to normal, but pain can be controlled and further damage slowed with the right care.',
    '<b>Should I stop exercising if my joints hurt?</b><br>Do not stop completely, because not moving your joints at all can weaken the muscles around them. Choose low-impact exercise such as swimming or walking.',
    '<b>Can young people get osteoarthritis?</b><br>Yes, especially people who have had a joint injury or who use their joints heavily and repeatedly, although it is more common in older adults.',
    '<b>Do joint supplements really help?</b><br>Results vary from person to person. Talk to a doctor before taking supplements alongside your main treatment.',
    'Summary',
    'Osteoarthritis is the most common joint disease, caused by the cartilage in the joints wearing away with use and age. Although it cannot be completely reversed, early care — suitable exercise, weight control and seeing a doctor when you have symptoms — helps slow it down and control pain much better.',
    'References',
    None,
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. See a doctor if you have ongoing joint pain or it interferes with daily life.</i>',
  ]),
}
